2. Background and Related Work

2.1 Geometric Deep Learning

Geometric Deep Learning provides a general framework for exploiting structural regularities such as grids, groups, and manifolds in machine learning [Bronstein et al., 2021]. This is a foundational pillar for MGWU, as it provides the mathematical language to build inductive biases that reflect the structure of the physical world. Rather than learning everything from data, the architecture should be designed to exploit known symmetries and relations. In MGWU, each geometry is formalized as a Riemannian manifold whose metric encodes domain‑specific structure.

2.2 The JEPA Lineage and a Critical Distinction

The most direct living competitor to the MGWU framework is LeCun’s Joint Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA) and its subsequent implementations [LeCun, 2022; Assran et al., 2023; Assran et al., 2025]. The stated goal of JEPA is to learn abstract, predictive representations of the world by predicting the representation of an input in a unified latent space. The V‑JEPA 2 model, described in [Assran et al., 2025], has demonstrated that such a system can learn a physical world model from unlabeled video and, in its action‑conditioned variant (V‑JEPA 2‑AC), transfer this knowledge to control a real robot arm. This represents a formidable empirical milestone.

MGWU differs fundamentally in its structural assumption. JEPA, and similar architectures like Dreamer [Hafner et al., 2023] and MuZero [Schrittwieser et al., 2020], implicitly assume that a single, unified representational space is sufficient to capture all relevant structure. MGWU posits that this is not the case. We propose that a world model becomes more robust and capable of higher‑order reasoning (causal, counterfactual) when it is built from a family of explicitly distinct geometries, each with its own native structure, that are constrained to be mutually consistent via a shared dynamical core.

The core question this paper poses to the JEPA paradigm is: Can a single hierarchical predictive space learn to represent, for example, both the continuous symmetries of physical dynamics and the discrete, relational structure of causality as effectively as two separate, specialized spaces that are constrained to align? MGWU’s central claim is that it cannot, and that explicit structural plurality—mediated by a low‑dimensional core that captures the shared dynamics—is the key to unlocking more robust forms of understanding.

Recent empirical and theoretical work has begun to test the limits of single‑space world models. Causal‑JEPA [Nam et al., 2026] is a direct response to the observation that standard JEPA models often rely on spurious correlations and fail to capture object‑level causal interactions. Causal‑JEPA introduces object‑level latent interventions within a JEPA backbone, using a causal masking strategy to induce a causal inductive bias. Evaluated on the CLEVRER benchmark, it achieves a roughly 20% absolute improvement in counterfactual‑reasoning accuracy over an architecture‑matched baseline. This work is both encouraging and cautionary for MGWU: it demonstrates that even within a single‑space paradigm, explicit causal mechanisms improve performance. However, Causal‑JEPA remains a monolithic architecture; the causal structure is induced via masking, not represented as a separate geometry with its own relational metric. MGWU’s claim is that a fully separate causal geometry, aligned with other geometries through a shared core, can capture a richer form of causal understanding—one that supports not only counterfactual prediction but also explicit causal discovery and graph‑based reasoning. In Section 6 we elaborate on this distinction and propose an experimental comparison using CLEVRER as a benchmark, thereby inheriting a real baseline number to beat.

Additionally, recent theoretical advances in causal representation learning (CRL) provide formal guarantees that a latent causal graph can be recovered from interventional data with relatively few environments [Lee, Jin & Aragam, 2026]. This is directly relevant to our design of a causal geometry, as we discuss in §2.3 and §6.

2.3 Causal Representation Learning

Causal representation learning (CRL) attempts to recover meaningful latent variables and their causal relationships from high‑dimensional observations [Schölkopf et al., 2021]. A central mathematical challenge is that the observed variables may be mixtures of latent variables whose causal structure is not directly visible. Recent work has established strong identifiability guarantees under interventions. Lee, Jin, and Aragam [2026] prove that with a logarithmic number of environments (relative to the number of latent nodes) and two stochastic hard interventions per node, the latent causal graph, the causal representations, and the decoder are identifiable up to a permutation and elementwise transformation. This result is directly relevant to MGWU because it demonstrates that an explicit causal geometry can, in principle, be learned from interventional data—a capability that is central to counterfactual reasoning.

MGWU explicitly incorporates a causal geometry G_C that is trained using interventional data, leveraging these identifiability results. This contrasts with approaches that attempt to infer causality from observational data alone, which are known to be severely underdetermined.

2.4 World Models

Recent surveys characterize world models as internal representations used either to represent mechanisms of the world or predict future states and dynamics [Hafner et al., 2023; Matsuo et al., 2024]. The distinction between predictive and mechanistic world models is precisely the distinction MGWU seeks to address. Prediction alone may be a statistical shortcut; mechanistic understanding requires modeling interventions. We argue that a mechanistic world model requires multiple, structured representation spaces because the underlying mechanisms of the world—physical dynamics, causal relations, linguistic semantics—obey fundamentally different mathematical structures.

2.5 Modularity and Multi‑Expert Architectures

The idea of modularity in cognitive systems has a long history, from Fodor’s modularity of mind to modern mixture‑of‑experts models [Shazeer et al., 2017]. However, these approaches typically use a single shared representation space, with different modules operating on that space. MGWU differs in that each module is a distinct geometry with its own metric and relational structure. This is closer to the idea of heterogeneous representation spaces that must be aligned, as seen in multi‑modal foundation models like CLIP [Radford et al., 2021]. CLIP aligns two spaces (language and vision) via contrastive learning, but it does not enforce structural consistency beyond pointwise alignment. MGWU generalizes this to multiple geometries with explicit structural constraints and a shared dynamical core.

