3. The Multi‑Geometric World Representation

We define an artificial world representation as a collection of structured spaces, called geometries, along with a shared dynamical core:

\mathcal{G} = \{ G_L, G_V, G_P, G_C, G_{Self} \}, \quad \mathcal{W}_{dyn}

where:

· G_L is the linguistic geometry,
· G_V is the visual/perceptual geometry,
· G_P is the physical/dynamical geometry,
· G_C is the causal geometry,
· G_{Self} is the self‑model geometry,
· \mathcal{W}_{dyn} is a low‑dimensional dynamical core that supports prediction and planning.

The number of geometries is not fixed; the architecture permits additional representational geometries to emerge when the environment requires them. Each geometry G_i is a concrete mathematical structure, defined in Section 4. The dynamical core \mathcal{W}_{dyn} is a shared latent space that captures the state of the world in a format suitable for temporal prediction and action selection.

Crucially, the geometries are not independent; they are coupled through (i) cross‑geometric alignment constraints and (ii) projection mappings to and from the dynamical core. This coupling ensures that the system maintains a coherent world model despite the heterogeneity of its representational spaces.

