4. An Operational Definition of a Geometry

In this framework, a “geometry” is instantiated as a Riemannian manifold equipped with an encoder and a derived relational structure. Formally:

G_i = (\mathcal{M}_i, g_i, f_i, \mathcal{R}_i)

where:

· \mathcal{M}_i is a smooth manifold (the representation space),
· g_i is a Riemannian metric tensor on \mathcal{M}_i,
· f_i: \mathcal{O} \rightarrow \mathcal{M}_i is a learned encoder that maps an observation o \in \mathcal{O} to a point x_i \in \mathcal{M}_i,
· \mathcal{R}_i is a derived relational structure (e.g., a similarity graph) constructed from pairwise distances induced by g_i.

The distance function d_i is the geodesic distance on \mathcal{M}_i under the metric g_i, though for computational tractability we often approximate it by the ambient Euclidean or cosine distance when the manifold is embedded in a vector space.

We define \mathcal{R}_i as a similarity graph constructed on a batch of N representations B = \{x_i^{(1)}, \dots, x_i^{(N)}\}. An edge exists between x_i^{(a)} and x_i^{(b)} if d_i(x_i^{(a)}, x_i^{(b)}) < \epsilon_i, where \epsilon_i is a geometry‑specific threshold (e.g., the 10th percentile of pairwise distances in the batch).

For a toy environment, we can specify the geometries explicitly:

· G_L (Linguistic): \mathcal{M}_L is a hyperbolic space \mathbb{H}^d (e.g., the Poincaré ball) to capture hierarchical semantic structure. f_L is a pre‑trained language model encoder; d_L is the hyperbolic distance; \mathcal{R}_L captures semantic similarity.
· G_V (Visual): \mathcal{M}_V is the feature space of a convolutional network (e.g., ResNet) embedded in Euclidean space. d_V is Euclidean distance; \mathcal{R}_V captures perceptual similarity.
· G_P (Physical): \mathcal{M}_P is the cotangent bundle T^*\mathcal{Q} of the configuration manifold \mathcal{Q} of the scene, endowed with a symplectic structure (see §4.1 for full formalization). d_P is a geodesic distance combining Lie group distances on \mathrm{SE}(3) and momentum differences; \mathcal{R}_P captures physical state similarity.
· G_C (Causal): \mathcal{M}_C is the space of node embeddings of a Graph Neural Network; d_C is a graph‑theoretic distance (e.g., shortest path length in the learned causal graph); \mathcal{R}_C is the learned directed acyclic graph (DAG) representing direct causal influence.
· G_{Self} (Self‑model): \mathcal{M}_{Self} is a Euclidean space of meta‑cognitive features (e.g., reliability estimates for each geometry, uncertainty measures); d_{Self} is Euclidean distance; \mathcal{R}_{Self} captures similarity in the system’s self‑assessment.

4.1 Formalization of the Physical Geometry G_P on \mathrm{SE}(3) and Phase Space Manifolds

To overcome the expressivity limitations of ambient Euclidean spaces, we formalize the Physical Geometry G_P as a Riemannian phase space manifold equipped with symplectic structures and Lie group symmetries. Physical objects do not merely exist as arbitrary vector embeddings; their kinematics and dynamics strictly adhere to continuous Euclidean transformations and energy conservation principles.

Formally, the Physical Geometry is defined as the tuple:

G_P = \left( \mathcal{M}_P, g_P, f_P, d_P, \mathcal{R}_P \right)

4.1.1 Configuration Manifold and Lie Group Symmetries

Consider a scene composed of K rigid objects. The spatial configuration of each object k \in \{1, \dots, K\} is governed by the Special Euclidean Group \mathrm{SE}(3) = \mathbb{R}^3 \rtimes \mathrm{SO}(3), representing rigid translations and rotations in 3D space.

A pose element g_k \in \mathrm{SE}(3) is represented in homogeneous coordinates as:

g_k = \begin{pmatrix} R_k & p_k \\ 0_{1 \times 3} & 1 \end{pmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{4 \times 4}, \quad R_k \in \mathrm{SO}(3), \; p_k \in \mathbb{R}^3

The configuration space \mathcal{Q} of the physical scene is the product manifold of individual poses:

\mathcal{Q} = \prod_{k=1}^K \mathrm{SE}(3)

The infinitesimal motion of an object is captured by its twist vector \xi_k = (v_k, \omega_k)^T \in \mathbb{R}^6 in the Lie algebra \mathfrak{se}(3) \cong T_e \mathrm{SE}(3), mapped to matrix exponential coordinates via the logarithmic map:

\xi_k = \log_{\mathrm{SE}(3)}(g_k^{-1} g_k')

4.1.2 Phase Space Manifold and Symplectic Structure

To capture dynamic evolution (mass, velocity, forces, and momentum) alongside static poses, \mathcal{M}_P is instantiated as the cotangent bundle (phase space) of the configuration manifold:

\mathcal{M}_P = T^*\mathcal{Q} \cong \mathcal{Q} \times \mathfrak{q}^*

A complete state representation x_P \in \mathcal{M}_P for K bodies is defined by canonical coordinate‑momentum pairs:

x_P = (q, p) = \left( (g_1, \dots, g_K), \, (p_1, \dots, p_K) \right) \in T^*\mathcal{Q}

where q \in \mathcal{Q} denotes generalized spatial configurations and p \in T_q^*\mathcal{Q} denotes canonical linear and angular momenta.

The phase space \mathcal{M}_P is equipped with a canonical symplectic 2‑form \omega = \sum_{i} dq^i \wedge dp_i. Under this structure, physical dynamics are governed by a learned Hamiltonian vector field X_H satisfying Hamilton’s equations:

\iota_{X_H} \omega = dH \quad \implies \quad \dot{q} = \frac{\partial H}{\partial p}, \quad \dot{p} = -\frac{\partial H}{\partial q}

where H(q, p) = T(q, p) + V(q) represents the total system energy (kinetic energy T parameterized by mass‑inertia matrix \mathbf{M}(q), and potential energy V(q)). Enforcing symplectic symmetry in f_P guarantees that energy and phase space volume (Liouville’s theorem) are strictly preserved under deterministic forward dynamics in G_P.

4.1.3 Riemannian Metric and Geodesic Distance d_P

The metric tensor g_P on \mathcal{M}_P is constructed by combining the left‑invariant Riemannian metric on \mathrm{SE}(3)—weighted by the diagonal spatial mass‑inertia matrix \mathbf{M}(q) \succ 0—with the dual metric on canonical momenta.

For two physical states x_P^{(a)} = (q_a, p_a) and x_P^{(b)} = (q_b, p_b) in \mathcal{M}_P, the geodesic distance metric d_P\left(x_P^{(a)}, x_P^{(b)}\right) is defined as:

d_P\left(x_P^{(a)}, x_P^{(b)}\right)^2 = d_{\mathrm{SE}(3)}^2(q_a, q_b) + \left\lVert p_a - p_b \right\rVert_{\mathbf{M}^{-1}}^2

where the Lie group distance d_{\mathrm{SE}(3)} between multi‑body pose configurations q_a and q_b is evaluated using the Lie algebra norm:

d_{\mathrm{SE}(3)}^2(q_a, q_b) = \sum_{k=1}^K \left\lVert \log_{\mathrm{SE}(3)}\left(g_{k,a}^{-1} g_{k,b}\right) \right\rVert_{\mathbf{W}}^2

with symmetric positive‑definite weighting tensor \mathbf{W} = \text{diag}(m_k I_3, \mathbf{I}_k) encoding object mass m_k and rotational inertia tensor \mathbf{I}_k.

4.1.4 Encoder Mapping and Derived Relational Structure

The physical encoder f_P: \mathcal{O} \to \mathcal{M}_P maps raw visual/sensory observations o \in \mathcal{O} into explicit manifold coordinates:

f_P(o) = \hat{x}_P = (\hat{q}, \hat{p}) = \left( \{(\hat{R}_k, \hat{p}_k)\}_{k=1}^K, \{(\hat{p}_{v,k}, \hat{p}_{\omega,k})\}_{k=1}^K \right)

The derived relational structure \mathcal{R}_P is constructed on a batch of N physical states as an energy‑bounded interaction graph \mathcal{R}_P = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}_P):

\mathcal{E}_P = \left\{ (a, b) \;\middle|\; d_P\left(x_P^{(a)}, x_P^{(b)}\right) < \epsilon_P \quad \text{and} \quad \left| H\left(x_P^{(a)}\right) - H\left(x_P^{(b)}\right) \right| < \delta_E \right\}

where \epsilon_P is the physical metric distance threshold and \delta_E enforces energy manifold equivalence.

This formulation ensures that G_P natively respects \mathrm{SE}(3) equivariance under rigid coordinate transformations while embedding Hamiltonian energy conservation directly into the physical geometry’s metric space.

