7. The Self‑Model Geometry: A Differentiable Router for Meta‑Cognitive Control

The self‑model geometry G_{Self} is designed to enable the system to monitor and control its own cognitive processes. In earlier drafts, G_{Self} was vaguely defined as summarizing internal state and uncertainty. Here, we give it a concrete functional role: geometry routing.

Given a query (e.g., a counterfactual question), the system must decide which geometry (or combination of geometries) is most reliable for answering. Previous formulations relied on an oracle label i^*_n indicating the correct geometry, which restricted G_{Self} to synthetic environments where ground‑truth geometry selection is known a priori. To operate in fully self‑supervised or unsupervised real‑world domains, we formulate G_{Self} as an end‑to‑end differentiable Softmax Mixture‑of‑Geometries (MoG) router parameterized by a temperature \tau:

\alpha = \text{Softmax}\left( \frac{\text{MLP}_{Self}(z_{query}, z_{core})}{\tau} \right) \in \Delta^{|\mathcal{G}|}

\hat{y} = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{G}} \alpha_i \cdot \text{decoder}_i(z_i)

When training on the task loss \mathcal{L}_{task}, task gradients \nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_{task} backpropagate directly through the weighting coefficients \alpha_i. To prevent the router from prematurely collapsing onto a single geometry, we add an entropy regularization penalty:

\mathcal{L}_{self\_ent} = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{G}} \alpha_i \log \alpha_i

This encourages the router to explore multiple geometries during training while still allowing it to converge to a sharp distribution if one geometry is consistently superior for a given query type. The total self‑model loss is:

\mathcal{L}_{self} = \mathcal{L}_{task} + \lambda_{ent} \, \mathcal{L}_{self\_ent}

Additionally, G_{Self} can be used to detect when the cross‑geometric alignment is failing (e.g., when the geometries disagree significantly) and to trigger a re‑alignment procedure.

