10. Conclusion

The Multi‑Geometric World Understanding Hypothesis proposes a shift from monolithic, unified latent spaces toward a family of explicitly distinct, structurally‑specialized geometries that are constrained to be mutually consistent via a shared dynamical core. This framework directly confronts the dominant JEPA paradigm, arguing that a plurality of inductive biases is a more powerful and testable path toward robust world models.

This paper has moved the framework from a philosophical proposal to an operational research program. By defining geometries concretely as Riemannian manifolds (with a detailed symplectic treatment of the physical geometry), proposing a scale‑invariant star‑topology cross‑geometric alignment loss, incorporating causal representation learning, and replacing the oracle‑based router with a differentiable mixture‑of‑geometries mechanism, we provide a clear path for empirical validation. The ultimate goal is not to program consciousness directly, but to investigate whether sufficiently rich integration of representations can produce progressively deeper forms of artificial understanding. The experiment proposed here is the first, critical step on that path.


Appendix A: On the Question of Consciousness

The MGWU framework makes no claims regarding phenomenal consciousness. It proposes a scientifically testable mechanism for advanced forms of understanding, causal reasoning, and self‑modeling. Whether the recursive integration of such models could ever produce subjective experience is a deeply important question, but it is beyond the scope of the testable scientific claims made in this paper. We explicitly distance our empirical hypotheses from any theory of consciousness to maintain a clear boundary between what can be operationalized and tested, and what remains a matter of philosophical and empirical debate.

