Appendix C: Formal Geometry Definitions and Metrics

We provide a complete formalization of each geometry as a Riemannian manifold or metric space with an associated encoder and relational structure.

C.1 Linguistic Geometry G_L

Manifold: \mathcal{M}_L = \mathbb{H}^d, the d‑dimensional hyperbolic space (Poincaré ball model) with curvature -1.
Metric tensor: g_L(u,v) = \frac{4 \langle u, v \rangle}{(1 - \lVert u \rVert^2)^2} for tangent vectors u,v at a point x \in \mathbb{H}^d.
Encoder: f_L: \mathcal{O} \to \mathbb{H}^d is a pre‑trained language model (e.g., Sentence‑BERT) followed by an exponential map from Euclidean embeddings to the Poincaré ball.
Distance: d_L(x, y) = \operatorname{arcosh}\left(1 + 2\frac{\lVert x - y \rVert^2}{(1 - \lVert x \rVert^2)(1 - \lVert y \rVert^2)}\right).
Relational structure: \mathcal{R}_L is a similarity graph with edge if d_L(x^{(a)}, x^{(b)}) < \epsilon_L, where \epsilon_L is the 10th percentile of pairwise distances in the batch.

C.2 Visual Geometry G_V

Manifold: \mathcal{M}_V = \mathbb{R}^{d_V}, Euclidean space.
Metric tensor: g_V = I_{d_V}, the standard Euclidean metric.
Encoder: f_V is a convolutional neural network (e.g., ResNet‑50) outputting a d_V-dimensional feature vector.
Distance: d_V(x, y) = \lVert x - y \rVert_2.
Relational structure: \mathcal{R}_V is a similarity graph with edge if d_V(x^{(a)}, x^{(b)}) < \epsilon_V.

C.3 Physical Geometry G_P

Manifold: \mathcal{M}_P = T^*\mathcal{Q}, the cotangent bundle of the configuration manifold \mathcal{Q} = \prod_{k=1}^K \mathrm{SE}(3), equipped with the canonical symplectic form \omega = \sum_{i} dq^i \wedge dp_i.
Metric tensor: g_P is constructed from the left‑invariant metric on \mathrm{SE}(3) weighted by mass‑inertia matrix \mathbf{M}(q), plus the dual metric on momenta.
Encoder: f_P maps observations to explicit pose and momentum coordinates (q, p).
Distance: d_P\left((q_a,p_a), (q_b,p_b)\right)^2 = d_{\mathrm{SE}(3)}^2(q_a,q_b) + \lVert p_a - p_b \rVert_{\mathbf{M}^{-1}}^2, with d_{\mathrm{SE}(3)} as defined in §4.1.3.
Relational structure: \mathcal{R}_P is an energy‑bounded interaction graph with edge conditions given in §4.1.4.

C.4 Causal Geometry G_C

Space: \mathcal{M}_C = \mathbb{R}^{d_C}, the space of node embeddings from a GNN.
Metric: d_C(x, y) is the shortest directed path length in the learned causal DAG A; if no path exists, d_C = d_{\max} (a large constant).
Encoder: f_C is a graph neural network that maps observations to a set of latent node embeddings z_C.
Relational structure: \mathcal{R}_C is the directed acyclic graph with weighted adjacency matrix A \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}, learned via NOTEARS.

C.5 Self‑Model Geometry G_{Self}

Manifold: \mathcal{M}_{Self} = \mathbb{R}^{d_{Self}}, Euclidean space.
Encoder: f_{Self} maps the query and core state to a meta‑cognitive feature vector.
Distance: d_{Self}(x, y) = \lVert x - y \rVert_2.
Relational structure: \mathcal{R}_{Self} captures similarity in the system’s self‑assessment, e.g., reliability estimates for each geometry.

