Appendix H: Physical Geometry: Symplectic Structure and Hamiltonian Dynamics

H.1 Symplectic Form and Hamiltonian Flow

The phase space \mathcal{M}_P = T^*\mathcal{Q} is equipped with the canonical symplectic form \omega = \sum_i dq^i \wedge dp_i. The Hamiltonian vector field X_H satisfies \iota_{X_H}\omega = dH, leading to Hamilton’s equations:

\dot{q} = \frac{\partial H}{\partial p}, \quad \dot{p} = -\frac{\partial H}{\partial q}

Theorem 5 (Energy conservation). Along trajectories of X_H, the Hamiltonian H(q(t), p(t)) is constant.

Proof. Using the chain rule and Hamilton’s equations,

\frac{dH}{dt} = \frac{\partial H}{\partial q} \cdot \dot{q} + \frac{\partial H}{\partial p} \cdot \dot{p} = \frac{\partial H}{\partial q} \cdot \frac{\partial H}{\partial p} - \frac{\partial H}{\partial p} \cdot \frac{\partial H}{\partial q} = 0.

∎

H.2 Geodesic Distance on \mathrm{SE}(3)

The Lie algebra \mathfrak{se}(3) has the matrix representation of twists. The logarithm map \log_{\mathrm{SE}(3)}: \mathrm{SE}(3) \to \mathfrak{se}(3) gives the twist vector \xi = (v, \omega). For two poses g_1, g_2 \in \mathrm{SE}(3), the left‑invariant distance is:

d_{\mathrm{SE}(3)}(g_1, g_2) = \left\lVert \log_{\mathrm{SE}(3)}(g_1^{-1} g_2) \right\rVert_{\mathbf{W}}

where \mathbf{W} is a positive‑definite weighting matrix. This distance is bi‑invariant if \mathbf{W} is chosen appropriately (e.g., proportional to the identity on translations and rotations).

