Appendix I: Training Pseudocode and Complexity Analysis

I.1 Training Loop

```
for epoch in range(num_epochs):
    for batch in dataloader:
        # Encode observations into each geometry
        z_L = encoder_L(batch.text)
        z_V = encoder_V(batch.image)
        z_P = encoder_P(batch.video)   # outputs (q, p)
        z_C = encoder_C(batch.video)   # outputs node embeddings and adjacency A
        z_core = aggregate([e_L(z_L), e_V(z_V), e_P(z_P), e_C(z_C)])

        # Compute losses
        loss_task = cross_entropy(decoder(z_core, query), batch.answer)
        loss_cgc, loss_reg = alignment_loss(geometries, z_core)
        loss_causal = reconstruction_loss(z_C, batch) + intervention_loss(A, batch) + dag_constraint(A)
        weights, entropy = router(query, z_core)
        loss_self = loss_task + lambda_ent * entropy

        total_loss = loss_task + lambda_1*loss_cgc + lambda_2*loss_causal + lambda_3*loss_self + lambda_4*loss_reg
        total_loss.backward()
        optimizer.step()
```

I.2 Complexity of Alignment Loss

For K geometries and batch size N, the all‑pairs alignment cost is \mathcal{O}(K^2 N^2) per iteration. The star‑topology reduces this to \mathcal{O}(K N^2). For K=5, N=128, the star topology is about 5\times cheaper.

